<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">al-Nasir Dawud</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1206–1258</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">al-Nasir Dawud was an Ayyubid prince, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-muazzam-isa">al-Mu'azzam Isa</a>. He briefly ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> from 1227 until his uncles took it in 1229, after which he held the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-karak">Kerak</a> east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea">Dead Sea</a> as his principality. A noted poet and scholar, he spent decades in shifting alliances among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> and lost Kerak to his own sons in the 1240s.</p></div>
